@ 14.3.1 - Standard Tamil to Colloquial Tamil
# citation: schwatever, from Shiffman, Harold F. A Reference Grammar on Spoken Tamil “There’s also only a few changes necessary to turn this into the British dialect (which didn’t merge retroflexes with alveolars):”
	aj > e:[+long] “(exception: never finally in monosyllables, never initially in multisyllabic words)”
	avu aji > aw aj
	i u > e o / _Ca
	{k,v} > ∅ / V_V
	a:[+long] e:[+long] i:[+long] o:[+long] u:[+long] > a ɛ i o u / _#
	am an {a:[+long]m,a:[+long]n} > õ æ̃ ã / _#
	{om,on} {em,en} {o:[+long]m,o:[+long]n} {e:[+long]m,e:[+long]n} > ɔ̃ ɛ̃ õ ẽ
	um > ũ / _#
	∅ > ɨ / _N#
	{ɭ,ɽ} > ∅ (sporadic, the latter very much so and contributing some compensatory lengthening)
	l ɭ > l:[+long]ʉ ɭ:[+long]ʉ / _#(C)V:[-long]
	∅ > ʉ / {l,ɭ}_# if {M,V:[+long]} previously in the lexeme
	r > ɾ “in most dialects”
	ɽ > ɭ
	{ɾ,l,ɭ} > ∅ / V_S
	i u > ɨ ʉ / short only when unstressed ! in #U
	ɨ ʉ > ∅ / ! _#
	∅ > {ɨ,ʉ} / to break up clusters
	n > ŋ / _{k,ɡ}
	{ i, i:[+long] } { e, e:[+long] } > { u, u:[+long] } { o, o:[+long] } / {m,v,p}_C̣
	j > ∅ / V:[-front]_#
	j > j:[+long]i / E_#
	t:[+long] nt > c:[+long] ɲc / {i,j}_
	ʈk > k:[+long]
	n t:[+long] > n̪ t̪:[+long]
	ɳ > n “(sporadic and dialect development)”
	ɭ > l “(again, sporadic)”
	c > s / _{a,o,u,e}
	c:[+long] > tʃ:[+long] “(most dialects)”
	o e > u i / _C{u,i} “(highly sporadic)”
	— { i, i:[+long] } { e, e:[+long] } > { u, u:[+long] } { o, o:[+long] } / _ɭ
	— eCə oCə > Ce:[+long] Co:[+long] / #_